  )

```


#### Interpretation

```{r qu_1a_03, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Convert to long format
pearson_list <- as.data.frame(as.table(pearson_corr))

# Remove duplicates and self-correlations
pearson_list <- pearson_list[pearson_list$Var1 != pearson_list$Var2, ]
pearson_list <- pearson_list[!duplicated(
  t(apply(pearson_list[,1:2], 1, sort))
), ]

# Absolute correlations
pearson_list$abs_corr <- abs(pearson_list$Freq)

# Stratify (order from weak to perfect)
pearson_list$strata <- cut(
  pearson_list$abs_corr,
  breaks = c(0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1),
  labels = c("Weak (0–0.3)",
             "Moderate (0.3–0.5)",
             "Strong (0.5–0.7)",
             "Very Strong (0.7–0.9)",
             "Near-Perfect (0.9–1)"),
  include.lowest = TRUE,
  ordered_result = TRUE
)

# Convert to factor explicitly with desired order
pearson_list$strata <- factor(
  pearson_list$strata,
  levels = c("Weak (0–0.3)",
             "Moderate (0.3–0.5)",
             "Strong (0.5–0.7)",
             "Very Strong (0.7–0.9)",
             "Near-Perfect (0.9–1)")
)

# Summary table
corr_summary <- as.data.frame(table(pearson_list$strata))

# Add percentages
corr_summary$Percentage <- round(
  corr_summary$Freq / sum(corr_summary$Freq) * 100, 2
)

```


```{r qu_1a_04, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Extract specific percentages for interpretation
strong_corr_percent <- corr_summary %>%
  filter(Var1 %in% c("Strong (0.5–0.7)", "Very Strong (0.7–0.9)", "Near-Perfect (0.9–1)")) %>%
  summarise(total = sum(Percentage)) %>%
  pull(total)

very_strong_percent <- corr_summary %>%
  filter(Var1 == "Very Strong (0.7–0.9)") %>%
  pull(Percentage)

near_perfect_percent <- corr_summary %>%
  filter(Var1 == "Near-Perfect (0.9–1)") %>%
  pull(Percentage)

weak_percent <- corr_summary %>%
  filter(Var1 == "Weak (0–0.3)") %>%
  pull(Percentage)

# Find strongest correlation pair
strongest_pair <- pearson_list %>%
  filter(abs_corr == max(abs_corr)) %>%
  slice(1)

strongest_var1 <- as.character(strongest_pair$Var1)
strongest_var2 <- as.character(strongest_pair$Var2)
strongest_corr <- round(strongest_pair$Freq, 3)

# Count of near-perfect pairs
near_perfect_count <- corr_summary %>%
  filter(Var1 == "Near-Perfect (0.9–1)") %>%
  pull(Freq)

```


```{r qu_1a_05, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Display correlation summary table
corr_summary %>%
  dplyr::arrange(match(Var1, levels(pearson_list$strata))) %>%
  kable(
    caption = "Distribution of Pearson Correlation Strengths",
    col.names = c("Correlation Strength", "Count", "Percentage (%)"),
    align = c("l", "c", "c"),
    digits = 2,
    booktabs = TRUE
  ) %>%
  kable_styling(
    full_width = FALSE,
    bootstrap_options = c("striped", "hover", "condensed", "responsive"),
    font_size = 12,
    position = "center"
  ) %>%
  row_spec(0, bold = TRUE) %>%
  column_spec(1, bold = TRUE)

```


The correlation heatmap uses Angles of Eigenvectors (AOE) ordering, which arranges variables so that those with similar correlation patterns are grouped together. This makes it easier to see clusters of highly correlated predictors. The Plotly heatmap also provides interactive functionality, allowing users to hover over any cell to view the exact variable names and correlation coefficient (r-value) for that specific pair.

Looking at the distribution of absolute correlation coefficients across all `r nrow(pearson_list)` unique variable pairs:

- Weak correlations (|r| < 0.3): `r weak_percent`% of pairs
- Strong to near-perfect correlations (|r| > 0.5): `r strong_corr_percent`% of pairs
- Very strong correlations (|r| = 0.7–0.9): `r very_strong_percent`% of pairs
- Near-perfect correlations (|r| > 0.9): `r near_perfect_percent`% of pairs (`r near_perfect_count` variable pairs)

The strongest correlation observed is `r strongest_corr` between `r strongest_var1` and `r strongest_var2`.

The AOE ordering clearly separates the variables into two main groups. The first group contains the project physical and financial variables (V-1 to V-8), which are fixed for each project. These show mostly weak to moderate correlations with each other.

The second group contains the economic variables and indices (V-11 to V-29) repeated across five time lags (Lag 1 to Lag 5). Within this group, the AOE ordering clusters together all lags of the same economic variable, and also places economically related variables (like exchange rates and liquidity) close to each other. This group shows very strong to near-perfect correlations, both within the same variable across different lags, and between different economic variables that are linked through macroeconomic conditions.

The AOE ordering also helps identify how the variables are structured. The economic variables repeat every 19 columns (V-11 to V-29) across lags 1 to 5, which explains why the heatmap shows clear block-like clusters. The near-perfect correlations occur because the same underlying economic indicator is measured at slightly shifted time points.

Because of this structure, there is substantial multicollinearity in the dataset. This means that standard linear regression would have unstable coefficients and inflated standard errors. Regularisation methods like Ridge, LASSO, or ElasticNet are more suitable here, as they can handle these strong correlations by shrinking coefficients or selecting relevant groups of predictors.


### Question One (B)

**(b) (8 marks) Please fit a linear regression model to explain the "actual sales price" (V104) in terms of the other variables excluding the variable "actual construction costs" (V105). Show and interpret the summary() output. Randomly split the data using a 80:20 split (train/test). Report the baseline test-set MSE.**


```{r qu_1b_01, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Create train/test split (80/20)
n <- nrow(q1_data)
q1_train_idx <- sample(1:n, size = 0.8 * n, replace = FALSE)

# Define raw train/test split
q1_X_train <- q1_data[q1_train_idx, !names(q1_data) %in% c("V104", "V105")]
q1_X_test  <- q1_data[-q1_train_idx, !names(q1_data) %in% c("V104", "V105")]
q1_y_train <- q1_data$V104[q1_train_idx]
q1_y_test  <- q1_data$V104[-q1_train_idx]

# Create full dataframes for train/test (easier to reference later)
q1_train <- q1_data[q1_train_idx, ]
q1_test  <- q1_data[-q1_train_idx, ]

```


```{r qu_1b_02, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Make train/test split summary table
q1_split_summary <- data.frame(
  Dataset = c("Training (80%)", "Test (20%)", "Total"),
  Observations = c(nrow(q1_X_train), nrow(q1_X_test), n)
)

kable(q1_split_summary, caption = "Train/Test Split Summary") %>%
  kable_styling(full_width = FALSE)

```


```{r qu_1b_03, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Fit linear regression model (V104 as response, exclude V105)
q1b_model <- lm(q1_y_train ~ ., data = as.data.frame(q1_X_train))

```


```{r qu_1b_04, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Model summary
summary(q1b_model)

```


```{r qu_1b_05, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Predict on test set and calculate MSE
q1b_pred <- predict(q1b_model, newdata = as.data.frame(q1_X_test))
q1b_mse <- mean((q1_y_test - q1b_pred)^2)

```

The baseline MSE value for the model is `r fmt(q1b_mse)`.

From the regression summary, several predictors have NA coefficient estimates. This occurs because strong multicollinearity among predictors leads to a singular model matrix. As identified in Question 1(A), many variable pairs exhibit very high correlations (|r| > 0.9), indicating substantial redundancy. During estimation, R automatically removes linearly dependent predictors, which results in undefined coefficient estimates appearing in the summary output.

Apart from that, several predictors were statistically significant at the 0.05 significance level, including V8 (p < 2e-16), COMPLETION YEAR (p = 1.38e-15), COMPLETION QUARTER (p = 1.32e-09), V5 (p = 0.000241), V1 (p = 0.004670), V4 (p = 0.030978), and V6 (p = 0.062074, borderline at the 0.10 level).


```{r qu_1b_06, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 10, fig.align = "center"}

# Partition the plot window, two rows and two columns.
par(mfrow = c(2, 2))

# Produce the diagnostic plots for model.
plot(q1b_model)

```


```{r qu_1b_07, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 7, fig.align = "center"}

# Create histogram of residuals
hist(
  residuals(q1b_model),
  breaks = 30,
  col = "lightblue",
  border = "white",
  main = "Histogram of Residuals for Question 1B Model",
  xlab = "Residual Values"
)

```

The diagnostic plots reveal several issues with the initial linear regression model. The Residuals vs Fitted plot shows a non-random pattern, suggesting potential non-linearity or omitted variables. The Normal Q-Q plot indicates deviations from normality, particularly in the tails, as the residuals do not follow the theoretical diagonal line closely. The Scale-Location plot shows some evidence of heteroscedasticity, with residuals spreading unevenly across fitted values. The histogram of residuals appears roughly centred around zero but shows some skewness and heavy tails. These diagnostic issues are expected given the severe multicollinearity identified in the correlation analysis. The presence of NA coefficients from linearly dependent predictors further confirms that the standard linear regression assumptions are violated, motivating the use of regularisation methods such as Ridge, LASSO, or ElasticNet in subsequent questions.


### Question One (C)

**(c) (8 marks) Using the same train/test split used in (b), fit a linear regression model to explain the "actual sales price" (V104) in terms of other variables (excluding the variable "actual construction costs" (V105)) using (i) backwards selection and (ii) stepwise selection. Compare the two models on: outputs, computational time (system.time), number of predictors kept, and test-set MSE.**


```{r v1_qu_1c_01, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Define null model (intercept only)
q1c_null <- lm(V104 ~ 1, data = q1_train)

# Define full model (all predictors except V105)
q1c_full <- lm(V104 ~ . - V105, data = q1_train)

```


```{r v1_qu_1c_02, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Train/test split summary table
q1c_split_summary <- data.frame(
  Dataset = c("Training (80%)", "Test (20%)", "Total"),
  Observations = c(nrow(q1_train), nrow(q1_test), n)
)

kable(q1c_split_summary, caption = "Train/Test Split Summary") %>%
  kable_styling(full_width = FALSE)

```


#### Question One (C.i.) - Backward Selection

```{r v1_qu_1c_03, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Backward selection using AIC
q1c_time_backward <- system.time({
  q1c_backward <- stepAIC(
    q1c_full,
    direction = "backward",
    trace = FALSE
  )
})

```

Backward selection completed in `r round(q1c_time_backward[3], 2)` seconds.


```{r v1_qu_1c_04, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Summary of backward model
summary(q1c_backward)

# Number of predictors for backward (excluding intercept)
q1c_backward_n <- length(coef(q1c_backward)) - 1

```


#### Question One (C.ii.) - Stepwise (Bidirectional) Selection

```{r v1_qu_1c_05, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Stepwise selection using AIC (both directions)
q1c_time_stepwise <- system.time({
  q1c_stepwise <- stepAIC(
    q1c_null,
    scope = list(lower = q1c_null, upper = q1c_full),
    direction = "both",
    trace = FALSE
  )
})

```

Stepwise selection completed in `r round(q1c_time_stepwise[3], 2)` seconds.


```{r v1_qu_1c_06, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Summary of stepwise model
summary(q1c_stepwise)

# Number of predictors (excluding intercept)
q1c_stepwise_n <- length(coef(q1c_stepwise)) - 1

```


#### Question One (C.iii.) - Comparison

```{r qu_1c_07, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Predictions on test set
q1c_pred_backward <- predict(q1c_backward, newdata = q1_test)
q1c_pred_stepwise <- predict(q1c_stepwise, newdata = q1_test)

# Test MSE calculations
q1c_mse_backward <- mean((q1_test$V104 - q1c_pred_backward)^2)
q1c_mse_stepwise <- mean((q1_test$V104 - q1c_pred_stepwise)^2)

```


```{r qu_1c_08, include = TRUE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}

# Create comparison table
q1c_comparison <- data.frame(
  Model = c("Backward Selection", "Stepwise (Bidirectional) Selection"),
  Predictors_Retained = c(q1c_backward_n, q1c_stepwise_n),
  Test_MSE = c(round(q1c_mse_backward, 2), round(q1c_mse_stepwise, 2)),
  Time_Seconds = c(round(q1c_time_backward[3], 2), round(q1c_time_stepwise[3], 2))
)

kable(q1c_comparison, caption = "Comparison of Variable Selection Methods") %>%
  kable_styling(full_width = FALSE, bootstrap_options = c("striped", "hover")) %>%
  row_spec(0, bold = TRUE)

```


The backward selection model retained `r q1c_backward_n` predictors and achieved a test MSE of `r fmt(q1c_mse_backward)`, completing in `r round(q1c_time_backward[3], 2)` seconds. The stepwise (bidirectional) selection model retained only `r q1c_stepwise_n` predictors with a test MSE of `r fmt(q1c_mse_stepwise)`, completing in `r round(q1c_time_stepwise[3], 2)` seconds.

The stepwise model achieved a substantially lower MSE (approximately `r round(100 * (q1c_mse_backward - q1c_mse_stepwise) / q1c_mse_backward, 1)`% lower) with less than half the number of predictors (`r q1c_stepwise_n` vs `r q1c_backward_n`), and was considerably faster to compute (`r round(q1c_time_stepwise[3], 2)` seconds vs `r round(q1c_time_backward[3], 2)` seconds).

Both backward and stepwise selection use greedy search algorithms that make locally optimal decisions at each step without evaluating all possible predictor subsets. However, stepwise selection offers greater flexibility because it allows both addition and removal of predictors at each stage. This bidirectional approach enables the algorithm to correct earlier decisions and potentially escape local optima that may trap the more restrictive backward selection method. This increased flexibility likely contributed to its superior performance, producing a more parsimonious model with better predictive accuracy.

Importantly, both selection methods produced models with dramatically lower test MSE compared to the full linear regression model from Question 1(B), which had an MSE of `r fmt(q1b_mse)`. The stepwise model reduced the MSE by approximately `r round(100 * (q1b_mse - q1c_mse_stepwise) / q1b_mse, 1)`%, demonstrating the value of variable selection in the presence of severe multicollinearity.


### Question One (D)

