The λ_min model selects `r length(q2c_sel_idx_min)` predictors, indicating that multiple aspects of speech contain useful information for predicting disease severity. In contrast, the λ_1se model selects only `r q2c_sel_feat_1se`, implying that much of the predictive signal can be captured by a single dominant feature. Note that the coefficient for X97 differs between models as stronger regularisation under λ_1se applies greater shrinkage. The exclusion of the remaining predictors suggests that much of the information contained in the full set of speech features is either redundant or weak once the selected variables are included. This is consistent with the presence of correlation among speech-derived features.

This leads to a clear trade-off between model complexity and predictive performance. The λ_min model achieves a lower test MSE (`r round(q2b_mse_min, 2)` vs `r round(q2b_mse_1se, 2)`), indicating better predictive accuracy, but at the cost of requiring measurement of multiple features. The λ_1se model is more parsimonious, requiring only a single predictor, which makes it easier and faster to implement in practice, particularly in clinical settings where time and resources may be limited.

Overall, the λ_min model would be preferred if predictive accuracy is the primary objective, as it achieves substantially lower test error. However, in settings where simplicity, interpretability, and data collection efficiency are prioritised, the λ_1se model provides a compelling alternative.


